    <text>
        <front>
            <div type="summary">
                <p>
                    Donald Brace clarifies the fourth clause on Mr Gaige's imprint and speaks of publishing conflicts. 
					Brace also discusses European and British copyright issues. He enquires after the publication date 
					in England.
                </p>
            </div>
        </front>
        <body>
            <div type="letter">
                <opener>
                    <dateline>
                        <placeName>
                            New York
                        </placeName>
                        <date when="1928-08-10">
                            10 August 1928
                        </date>
                    </dateline>
                    <address>
                        <addrLine>
                            <persName key="Q703935">Leonard Woolf</persName></addrLine>
							<addrLine>c/o <orgName key="Q1623362">The Hogarth Press</orgName>
                        </addrLine>
                        <addrLine>
                           Travistock Square 52</addrLine> <addrLine>London</addrLine>
                    </address>
                    <salute>
                        Dear Mr. Woolf:
                    </salute>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    I have your letter of <date when="1928-07-28">July 28th</date> referring to the <lb/> 
fourth clause in the agreement with <persName key="Q52156408">Mr. Gaige</persName>. Your <lb/>
understanding of Mr. Gaige’s intention is substantially <lb/>
correct. The entire edition will be printed with <persName>Mr. Gaige</persName>’s <lb/>
imprint, and of this edition <orgName key="Q34941688">Dulau</orgName> will take 200 copies for <lb/>
sale in <placeName key="Q21">England</placeName>. The fourth clause is intended to express <lb/>
two things: first, <persName>Mr. Gaige</persName>’s obligation to get this <lb/>
edition out so as not to interfere with or delay the <lb/>
publication of the book by you and ourselves; second, the <lb/>
requirement that I have insisted on, that his edition should <lb/>
do nothing to cloud the copyright. Technically, the limited <lb/>
edition must be published or sold simultaneously in the two <lb/>
countries; prior publication in <placeName key="Q30">America</placeName> alone would probably <lb/>
invalidate the British and European copyright, and it is <lb/>
my view that if <orgName>Dulau</orgName> puts copies on sale on the same day that <lb/>
<persName>Mr. Gaige</persName> publishes in <placeName>America</placeName>, the copyright will be <lb/>
effective in both countries. I think this is important for <lb/>
all of us. The situation is, therefore, that there will be <lb/>
no publication in <placeName>England</placeName> in the sense of having a publisher <lb/>
and a separate imprint and all that, but that sales will take <lb/>
place as a protection to you.</p>

	<p>To this limited extent there is a question about <lb/>
the date of publication in <placeName>England</placeName>, and it seems to me an <lb/>
important one. I hope this explains the matter.
                </p>
                <closer>
                    <salute>
                        Yours sincerely,
                    </salute>
                    <signed>Donald C. Brace</signed>
                    <seg type="postscript">
                        DCB.
                    </seg>
					<address>
                        <addrLine>
